Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan (@th-yan: 0, is #reference, #part-of @th-sources) { r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW
Claire Yu Yan. Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics, BCcampus, 2022. [Complete book and download choices](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/).

Read all six chapters, the relevant property tables and substantial exercises. The progression develops systems and properties, pure substances, gases, closed and open energy balances, entropy and the second law. It is an introductory one-term text; continue with [Engineering Thermodynamics — Olivier Cleynen](Engineering-Thermodynamics-Cleynen.lingua#r_38CM7HKS0GSN5T09YDXFKHRVGQ) for full power-cycle development and [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1) for deeper classical theory.

The publisher gives 1 September 2022 as publication date; its version history records creation on 31 August. The text is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 except noted components. Online interactive questions are not necessarily present in a static PDF. Preserve the actual download's version and attributions. Source text, images and exercises remain external.

**Return:** [Thermodynamics and heat transfer sources](../Sources.lingua#r_4N96T70VYW3C3M91M2SXF5KEQF).

**Third physics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** rechecked the official contents, author/date and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0/component notice. Selected definitions and arguments were read in [1.2, system and surroundings](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/system-and-surroundings/), [1.4, state/process/cycle](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/state-process-and-cycle/), [2.2, thermodynamic properties](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/thermodynamic-properties-of-a-state/), [3.1, the ideal-gas equation](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/3-1-ideal-gas-and-ideal-gas-equation-of-state/), [4.3, work](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/4-3-work/) and [4.4, the closed-system first law](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/4-5-the-first-law-for-closed-system/). Use the fixed-matter closed-system definition and keep equilibrium-path assumptions in pressure-volume work. The broader complete chapters, tables and interactive questions remain assignments rather than claims of a fresh complete audit. The new three-path example uses original stipulated states and independently checked accounts, not a reconstruction of a performed process. Source figures, text and exercises remain external.


**Sixth physics-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the publisher contents, author, September 2022 publication metadata and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0/component notice. Selected sections [6.3](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/6-3-the-second-law-kevin-planck-and-clausius-statements/), [6.4](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/6-4-carnot-cycles/), [6.5](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/6-5-entropy-and-entropy-generation/), [6.6](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/6-6-the-second-law-entropy-balance-equation-for-closed-systems/) and [6.7](https://pressbooks.bccampus.ca/thermo1/chapter/6-7-determining-the-specific-entropy-of-a-state/) were inspected for second-law, reversible-cycle, entropy/boundary and constant-property assumptions. The common lessons do not infer maximum power from the source's reversible-efficiency discussion, or a universal inequality between boundary and system temperatures from its boundary-temperature emphasis. Section 6.7.3 Example 3 was numerically reconstructed using its supplied air constants and temperature/pressure endpoints: −0.388505098839768… kJ/(kg K), agreeing with its −0.3885 rounding. Independent rational logarithm enclosures checked the result. Full chapters, figures, tables, interactive questions and experiments were not wholly audited or reproduced. Original finite-body and gas investigations remain hypothetical; earlier assignments and source media stay external.

